<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valladolid</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valladolid is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> at the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisuerga">Pisuerga</a> and Esgueva rivers, capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León. Developed by Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ansurez">Pedro Ansúrez</a> in the late 11th century, it became a favoured residence of the Castilian court, the seat of a high court and a university. Ferdinand and Isabella married there in 1469, Philip II was born there, and it was briefly the capital of Spain from 1601 to 1606.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valladolid.jpg" alt="Valladolid" width="320" height="129" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Queninosta · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ciudad_de_Valladolid,_desde_el_aire_edited.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
